e  so the idea is if users would actually  use them  they could keep the data encrypted and  still use it with their normal tools  and then it would actually be secure and  safe even if you store it on your laptop  and lose it  however the issue is here all of these  tools we had to write ourselves  as egi archive it is it is  understandably kind of impossible to go  somewhere else and say  hey i've come up with this way of  dealing with data  and you add it to your code so the  question here became how can we take  something that already works but make it  into something that  might even be extended into the  ecosystem so that  a tool developer might come and say  here's a new file format i can add this  to my code  and and then it's a supported native  part of my tool  that's when the idea started of creating  a file  format that would formalize all of these  things in a way that the community  accepts  um so the first step in this project was  to identify  if this need that we saw is a real need  and so as we started to develop this  product um that's how  standards the terminology of standards  are used for in the g48  the first thing we had to do was to  create a product review committee  and the requirement was that it was  comprised of people from various work  streams  um to ensure that i'm not just  hitting my close friends and they say  it's a great idea so it has to be  accepted as a good idea across  many different work streams so we ended  up having someone from  abroad institute who had someone from  epfl in switzerland  and then also within the ebi from the  vcf  file format standard product review  committee  so they then have to listen to you  explain what you're trying to do  and actually agree that this is an idea  that has  usage for the entire community not just  for a very small niche  nobody would use it  so you work with them to develop a  description of what it is that you're  trying to set out to do  this will also have to have some initial  reviews by the regulatory and ethics  work stream  uh just to make sure that you're within  any regulatory environments  that might be affected um ethics it's  part of  the whole kind of work that we're doing  with human genome data  a security work stream is quite  important here  they were actually our our strongest  critics in the beginning simply saying  have you looked at this one have you  looked at homomorphic encryption have  you looked at secure third-party  computing  have you considered this so they were  very very strict in  having us do our preparation properly  which was um  actually pretty good and then one of the  things you also  have to prepare to do outreach and  partner engagement later on  um again so that the the standard  actually that's being  promoted throughout the community being  used  um there must also be two maintainers  who write  implementations and maintain the  standard throughout its lifetime  then you write a proposal once  everything has been signed off by the  various committees and  send it to the steering committee the  steering committee is literally a  cross-section of academia and industry  that's the leadership across all the  different work streams  they look at the documentation and vote  on it  so they also have to agree that this is  something worthwhile pursuing  and if it is accepted you then get some  administrative support so you'll start  having  a zoom meeting room start having regular  meetings and  etc so it  this is where the actual work takes  place once once the  steering committee voted to give you  that as a developing standard you then  try to get  people interested in the topic and  capable of  um contributing come together and you  have  regular meetings you develop a standard  you develop implementations  it's sort of an iterative process until  you're  till you're sort of happy with what you  have  you have some code you show it works you  have a document that lists out what the  standard should look like  and you have all the documentation you  then go back to the original  people who you collect who you had at  the beginning of this  journey security  then in this case that was very  important  they did a very very close and thorough  review of  of the standard we had actually picked  one of our product review committee  members from the security work stream so  they had a good idea  throughout the process of what we're  doing which made this a bit easier  but once they were happy regulatory and  ethics was not a huge deal  for a file format that's more important  than for example data discovery  and things like that  once you're past that and you think  you're ready  your product review committee has to  review it again  they they comment on it they have  experiences with your own standards they  support  etc and there's also an open review  where the public can go and and comment  criticize  uh whatever on the standard so again you  go back  you address those and that's an  iterative process  eventually you go back to the steering  committee you view a presentation  um they have seen all the documentation  and again  they vote yes or no  and if they vote yes it is approved as a  standard you then have standard version  1.0  and you're going towards trying to  outreach encourage adoption  usage etc um a quick word on the  numbering scheme that is being used  so you start out with 1.0  and it's pretty standard so minor fixes  would get you to 1.0.1  from a major but compatible update would  get you from 1.0 to 1.1  if there are too many changes which  would mean the next version of your  standard is incompatible  it breaks the compatibility then you  would go from version one  to version two but version one to  version two is treated as a whole new  standard so you have to go through the  whole process again and  explain and convince people that this is  actually a worthwhile  enterprise why break something that  people are already using etc  um in my case 